n-profit society Headquartered in Rio de Janeiro. Founded 1897.", "newValue": "The Brazilian Academy of Letters (Academia Brasileira de Letras), founded on July 20, 1897, stands as a beacon of Brazilian literary culture and intellectual discourse. Based in Rio de Janeiro, this institution has provided a platform for the nation’s most esteemed literary figures, including prominent members such as Machado de Assis, who served as its first president, and Jorge Amado, both of whom have significantly influenced Brazilian literature. The Academy plays a pivotal role in the preservation and promotion of the Portuguese language in Brazil, making it a cornerstone of national identity in the context of South America’s cultural landscape.

